# Line endings, settled once, because the alternative is what it was.
#
# This project is worked on from two Windows machines, and neither had anything to say about
# line endings: no .gitattributes here, and `core.autocrlf` set on one checkout and not on the
# other. The result is a file that is byte-identical in content showing up as modified on one
# machine and not the other, and a `git pull` that stops on "local changes would be overwritten"
# for edits nobody made. It is the commonest reason an update is frightening.
#
# `text=auto` lets git decide what is text, store it with LF in the repository, and hand each
# machine whatever its checkout wants in the working tree. Nothing about the working tree changes
# on the machine you are reading this on; what changes is that the two machines now agree.
* text=auto

# Written and read by Windows shells, where CRLF is not cosmetic.
*.ps1   text eol=crlf
*.cmd   text eol=crlf
*.bat   text eol=crlf

# Never to be touched: a line-ending rewrite inside one of these is corruption.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.pdf   binary
*.zip   binary
